<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!-- The app's component vocabulary (F-035). Before this file, App.xaml held ten brushes, nine
         converters, and exactly ONE keyed control style — so every button in the app was a
         hand-copied attribute set. 63 sites across 15 files repeat the same four themed attributes,
         which is why they drifted and why every previous fix had to be applied 63 times.

         WHAT THESE STYLES DO NOT REACH (review gate, 2026-08-05): two buttons are constructed in
         C# rather than markup — CaptionColorPickerWindow's palette swatches and SquadLaunchWindow's
         Remove — and they set their brushes via FindResource, not via a Style. They bind the same
         derived edge and are guarded by their own test, but a change made here does NOT reach them.
         Text inputs are a separate, deliberate gap: they keep the authored divider until stage 5.

         These styles own the THEMED attributes only: background, foreground, border. Padding,
         margin and font size stay at the call site, because those legitimately differ — a toolbar
         button and a dialog footer button are not the same size, and pretending otherwise would
         trade one bug for a layout sweep nobody asked for. -->

    <!-- ============================ THE TEMPLATE (F-068, v1.20) ============================

         Until now these styles set only resting colours and inherited their TEMPLATE from the OS.
         That template's state triggers are hardcoded literals with no resource binding:

             IsMouseOver == True   ->  Background #BEE6FD, BorderBrush #3C7FB1
             IsPressed   == True   ->  Background #C4E5F6
             IsEnabled   == False  ->  Background #F4F4F4, Foreground #838383

         #BEE6FD is Windows Aero light blue. Every button in this app has flashed it on hover, in
         every theme, for the app's whole life — confirmed by eye on a running v1.19 build. Under
         flatline, a theme whose entire purpose is carrying no meaning in colour, the pointer paints
         sky blue on it.

         WHY A TEMPLATE AND NOT Style.Triggers. Layering triggers on top would be smaller and would
         survive a WPF-UI package bump, and it cannot work: every one of those setters carries
         TargetName="border", so it targets a named element INSIDE the inherited template. A Style
         trigger on the Button's own Background sets a different object's property — not a
         precedence contest, a different element. Exactly one of the seven (Foreground on disabled)
         is reachable that way, and one of seven is not a fix.

         THE COST, stated rather than discovered later: owning this template means a future WPF-UI
         improvement to its Button template will not reach these buttons. There is no version of
         this that fixes the defect and avoids that.

         Hover and pressed reuse EXISTING palette slots. A twelfth slot would be a contract change
         and every user theme already on disk would need updating to supply it (invariant 6 — the
         contract does not grow). RowBg is one step up from Navy in every built-in; Divider is a
         further step. Disabled is opacity plus a muted label rather than a colour, because flatline
         has no hue to spend — the same reasoning F-032 settled for status colours. -->
    <!-- THE RANKS BELOW DO NOT DERIVE FROM THE IMPLICIT BUTTON STYLE, and that is the second half
         of owning the template. BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type Button}}" inherits the implicit
         style's Style.Triggers as well as its setters, and an inherited TRIGGER outranks a plain
         Setter in the derived style. So the implicit disabled trigger overwrote each rank's
         Background the moment a button disabled, and the fill never reached the template at all:
         a disabled Launch As rendered as muted text on bare row, with no cyan anywhere. Removing
         opacity from our own disabled trigger did not fix it, because ours was never the trigger
         doing it. Found at C1 by the builder reporting "only the text is what's staying".

         Every rank sets its own Template, Background, Foreground and border, so there is nothing
         left worth inheriting, only the triggers that were fighting us. -->
    <ControlTemplate x:Key="AppButtonTemplate" TargetType="Button">
        <Grid>
            <Border x:Name="Chrome"
                    Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                    BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                    BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                    SnapsToDevicePixels="True" />

            <!-- Hover and pressed are a translucent SHEEN over the fill, not a replacement for it.
                 The first version set Chrome's Background to RowBgBrush on hover, which is a
                 SURFACE colour: fine under a quiet navy button, catastrophic under a bright one.
                 Hovering a cyan CTA turned it dark navy, and the builder's report at C1 was that
                 the button dims to nearly nothing. It was not dimming; it was being replaced.

                 A sheen is relative, so one rule works for every fill the app has or will have:
                 cyan, amber, navy, magenta-edged, and transparent. It also survives a theme
                 nobody has written yet, which a fixed hover slot cannot.

                 Opacity is set STATICALLY on these two layers and the triggers toggle Visibility.
                 That is deliberate: an opacity animated by a trigger is what erased the disabled
                 fill earlier in this same cycle, and ButtonStateGateTests now forbids a trigger
                 from setting Opacity at all. A fixed alpha on a dedicated layer is a different
                 thing, and the layer is IsHitTestVisible=False so it never eats a click. -->
            <!-- The hover layer carries an OUTLINE as well as a tint, and the outline is the part
                 that actually reads. A white tint is weakest exactly where the fill is brightest:
                 10% white over cyan is almost nothing, which is what the builder saw at C1 after
                 the previous fix ("now it's not dimming at all"). Luminance-based feedback cannot
                 work equally on a near-white fill and a near-black one.
                 An outline appearing is structural. It reads the same on cyan, amber, navy and on
                 a transparent ghost button, and it does not depend on the fill's brightness at
                 all. The tint stays as a supporting cue and is stronger on press than on hover, so
                 the two states remain distinguishable. -->
            <Border x:Name="HoverSheen"
                    Background="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}"
                    BorderBrush="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}"
                    BorderThickness="1"
                    Opacity="0.22"
                    Visibility="Collapsed"
                    IsHitTestVisible="False" />
            <Border x:Name="PressSheen"
                    Background="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}"
                    BorderBrush="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}"
                    BorderThickness="1"
                    Opacity="0.38"
                    Visibility="Collapsed"
                    IsHitTestVisible="False" />

            <ContentPresenter x:Name="Content"
                              Margin="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                              HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center"
                              RecognizesAccessKey="True"
                              SnapsToDevicePixels="True" />
        </Grid>
        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="HoverSheen" Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
            </Trigger>
            <Trigger Property="IsPressed" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="PressSheen" Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
            </Trigger>
            <!-- Disabled mutes the label and KEEPS the fill. See the C1 note above: fading the
                 fill on a dark row does not read as unavailable, it reads as gone. -->
            <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource MutedTextBrush}" />
            </Trigger>
        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
    </ControlTemplate>

    <!-- Primary. Navy field, white text, cyan edge. Measured 9.39:1 in brand, 8.05:1 in midnight,
         4.90:1 in magenta-heat — this recipe was always fine. It is here so it stops being copied
         by hand, not because it was broken. -->
    <Style x:Key="PrimaryButtonStyle" TargetType="Button">
        <Setter Property="Template" Value="{StaticResource AppButtonTemplate}" />
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource NavyBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource CyanBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    </Style>

    <!-- Secondary — the recipe F-025, F-030 and F-031 are all about.

         It differs from primary in exactly one attribute: the border. That single difference is the
         entire defect. Secondary bound DividerBrush, and Navy == Bg in all three built-in themes,
         so the fill contributed zero separation and the whole affordance rested on a border
         measuring 1.26:1 (brand), 1.16:1 (midnight), 1.14:1 (magenta-heat) against WCAG 1.4.11's
         3:1 floor. It shipped in the default theme at about a fifth of the required contrast.

         InteractiveEdgeBrush is derived by ContrastGuard from whatever the active theme supplies,
         so this clears 3:1 under any theme including ones users authored — without adding a slot
         they would have to fill in, and without touching DividerBrush, which is still correct
         everywhere it draws a separator rather than a control boundary.

         F-032: the label was MutedTextBrush, the same token helper prose uses. Muted-vs-White is
         2.42:1 in brand and 1.00:1 under flatline, so colour could not carry "quiet" under a theme
         this app supports — a control label and the paragraph beside it became the same thing.
         Weight carries it now, and the derived edge above already marks control-vs-prose. Note the
         label was never illegible: Muted on Navy measures 6.88:1 and passes AA. The defect was
         semantic, not contrast. -->
    <Style x:Key="SecondaryButtonStyle" TargetType="Button">
        <Setter Property="Template" Value="{StaticResource AppButtonTemplate}" />
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource NavyBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Normal" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource InteractiveEdgeBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    </Style>


    <!-- Secondary, strong label. The sweep that consolidated these found THREE recipes, not two.
         Since F-032 all three share a white label and differ by edge and weight: quiet secondary
         (derived edge, Normal), primary (cyan edge), and this one (derived edge, SemiBold).

         It gets the same edge fix and keeps its white label. Folding it into the quiet secondary
         would have fixed the contrast bug by demoting a dialog's main action to muted text, which
         is a legibility trade nobody asked for. Three recipes existed; three styles name them. -->
    <Style x:Key="SecondaryStrongButtonStyle" TargetType="Button">
        <Setter Property="Template" Value="{StaticResource AppButtonTemplate}" />
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource NavyBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource InteractiveEdgeBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    </Style>

    <!-- Destructive — the third rank, added for the three sites v1.18 enumerates: Remove on the
         account row, Clear history, and the Stop all confirm. Assigned BY NAME, never by sweep.
         "Destructive by consequence" is unbounded on its face, and F-068's 61 un-migrated call
         sites are a different cycle's; a fourth button needing a judgement call is the signal to
         drop the work rather than widen it.

         WHY IT IS NOT A COLOUR. MagentaBrush is the obvious answer and it fails by measurement in
         two of the four shipped themes. Under magenta-heat the theme sets Magenta and Cyan to the
         SAME value, so a magenta-edged destructive button and a cyan-edged PrimaryButtonStyle
         render identically — the rank would not exist as a rank in a theme a user can pick today.
         Under flatline Magenta is the dark achromatic accent: it measures 3.73:1 against Navy where
         the derived edge already measures 3.03:1, a difference no reader can name, and a dim grey
         outline reads as disabled rather than dangerous. RowExpiredAccentBrush — the warning token
         v1.17 established across expired rows, idle chips and the compat banner — fails the mirror
         image of the same test, because under flatline it is the same value as Cyan. And Magenta is
         the CTA fill on 13 inline-styled surfaces, so it already means "primary action" in places;
         borrowing it here would hand one token two meanings, which is the defect the secondary rank
         above exists to describe.

         SO THE RANK IS A RELATIONSHIP, not a hue. Two axes, both of which survive greyscale, colour
         vision deficiency, and any JSON a user drops in the themes folder:

           edge    InteractiveEdgeBrush at BorderThickness 2 — DOUBLE every other rank in this file,
                   all of which set 1. The derived brush is also the only boundary token guaranteed
                   to clear WCAG 1.4.11's 3:1 under a theme nobody has authored yet, so the loudest
                   edge here is also the one a theme cannot quietly turn invisible.
           weight  Bold — one step above SecondaryStrongButtonStyle's SemiBold, which is itself one
                   step above the quiet secondary's Normal. Three ranks, three weights, in order.

         Navy fill and a white label, same as the two ranks above, and deliberately so. A
         destructive control has to be unmistakable, not inviting: an accent FILL would make Remove
         the loudest thing on an account row, which is the same cycle's Close-button defect wearing
         the opposite costume. -->
    <Style x:Key="DestructiveButtonStyle" TargetType="Button">
        <Setter Property="Template" Value="{StaticResource AppButtonTemplate}" />
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource NavyBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource InteractiveEdgeBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="2" />
    </Style>

    <!-- CTA — the cyan-filled call to action. 23 sites wanted this rank and none existed: 17 wrote
         CyanBrush + NavyBrush inline and 6 more wrote the same thing as raw #17D4FA + #0F1F31,
         which are brand's hexes for exactly those two tokens. Those six were frozen to brand and
         stayed cyan-on-navy under flatline; this rank un-freezes them.

         DARK LABEL, NOT WHITE, and it is not a preference. White on CyanBrush measures 1.77:1 in
         brand, 1.96:1 midnight, 3.29:1 magenta-heat, 1.36:1 flatline — under AA in every theme the
         app ships. Navy on CyanBrush measures 9.39 / 8.05 / 4.90 / 12.84 and clears it in every
         one. The 23 existing sites already use the dark label, so this rank changes nothing about
         how they look; it only stops them being written by hand. -->
    <Style x:Key="CtaButtonStyle" TargetType="Button">
        <Setter Property="Template" Value="{StaticResource AppButtonTemplate}" />
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource CyanBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource NavyBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
        <!-- BORDERLESS, and the fill is the boundary. Every one of the 23 sites this replaces sets
             BorderThickness="0" — these are solid blocks, not outlined controls. An earlier draft
             gave them a 1px edge to satisfy the rendered-edge gate and it was the wrong fix: WCAG
             1.4.11 asks whether the control is distinguishable from what is adjacent to it, and
             cyan on the page background measures 9.39 / 8.05 / 4.90 / 12.84. The fill answers the
             rule; a border would only be decoration, and decoration that changes 23 buttons. -->
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
    </Style>

    <!-- Accent action — Stop, Squad Launch, and the six other sites that were solid magenta blocks.

         THE MAGENTA MOVED FROM THE FILL TO THE EDGE, and the measurement forced it. A white label
         on MagentaBrush is 3.79:1 in brand, 4.16:1 midnight, 3.29:1 magenta-heat — under the 4.5:1
         floor this file holds everything else to. A dark label does not rescue it either: navy on
         magenta is 4.40 / 3.79 / 4.90 / 3.73, under AA in three of four. There is no label colour
         that makes a magenta FILL legible in the themes this app ships, which is why F-050 has an
         exemption rather than a fix.

         As an edge it passes cleanly. The label now sits on Navy at 16.66 / 15.79 / 16.09 / 17.45,
         and the magenta border measures 4.40 / 3.79 / 4.90 / 3.73 against the fill behind it —
         above WCAG 1.4.11's 3:1 in all four. Same brand signal, legible label, and it survives
         greyscale because the edge is a boundary rather than a meaning.

         SEMIBOLD IS LOAD-BEARING, not decoration. Under magenta-heat the theme sets Magenta and
         Cyan to the SAME value, so this rank's edge and PrimaryButtonStyle's edge render
         identically there. Weight is what keeps them apart, the same way it separates the two
         secondary ranks above. Bold stays exclusive to Destructive so the file's three-ranks-
         three-weights ordering still reads. -->
    <Style x:Key="AccentActionButtonStyle" TargetType="Button">
        <Setter Property="Template" Value="{StaticResource AppButtonTemplate}" />
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource NavyBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource MagentaBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    </Style>

    <!-- Warning — Recycle, Re-authenticate, and the memory-pressure recycle glyph. The warning
         token v1.17 established across expired rows, idle chips and the compat banner, now
         carrying the three buttons that ACT on those warnings.

         Dark label, for the same reason CtaButtonStyle has one: navy on the accent measures
         8.85 / 7.23 / 9.85 / 12.84 while white measures 1.88 / 2.19 / 1.63 / 1.36 — under AA in
         every theme the app ships. All three existing sites already use the dark label, so this
         changes nothing about how they look.

         Borderless, and the fill is the boundary: the accent against the page measures
         8.85 / 7.23 / 9.85 / 12.84, well past 1.4.11's 3:1. -->
    <Style x:Key="WarningButtonStyle" TargetType="Button">
        <Setter Property="Template" Value="{StaticResource AppButtonTemplate}" />
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource RowExpiredAccentBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource NavyBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
    </Style>

    <!-- Ghost — the quiet affordances that sit ON something rather than next to it: the tag's
         remove glyph, the compact/expand toggle, the set-main swatch, dismiss links. Transparent
         fill, no border, no chrome at all; the label is the entire control.

         WHY IT IS NOT MEASURED AS A BOUNDARY. There is no fill and no edge to measure, so
         1.4.11's non-text rule has nothing to govern here — this is text on whatever surface hosts
         it, and 1.4.3's text contrast is the rule that applies. The label binds WhiteBrush, which
         is measured against every surface it can land on by the sibling text pairs. A ghost rank
         that invented a fill to have something to measure would be adding chrome to satisfy a
         gate, which is the opposite of the point.

         Hover still reads: the shared template tints Chrome to RowBgBrush on IsMouseOver, so a
         transparent button gains a visible surface exactly when the pointer is over it, and loses
         it again after. That is the affordance these sites previously had no way to express. -->
    <Style x:Key="GhostButtonStyle" TargetType="Button">
        <Setter Property="Template" Value="{StaticResource AppButtonTemplate}" />
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
    </Style>

    <!-- ============================ INPUTS (F-029, F-049) ============================

         A text box IS a UI component under WCAG 1.4.11, exactly as much as a button is. Wave 5
         fixed buttons and left these on DividerBrush, which produced a visible inconsistency the
         close-out captured: under the flatline theme the Alerts panel shows a "Show" button with a
         clear outline sitting directly beside a webhook field with no boundary at all.

         Same derived brush, same reasoning. This is not new policy — it is the policy wave 5 wrote,
         applied to the controls it did not reach. What stays on DividerBrush is decoration: card
         edges, row rules, the popup edge on a context menu. Those are separators, and 1.4.11 does
         not govern separators. The fence test enforces that split by role. -->

    <!-- NO Background setter, and that is deliberate. Two fills are in use and both are right:
         RowBgBrush where the field sits on a card (Export, Import, Games) and NavyBrush where it
         sits on window chrome (Preferences). A field takes its fill from the surface behind it, so
         forcing one here would flatten a real distinction — the same reason the button styles leave
         padding and font size at the call site. The border is what drifted; the border is what this
         owns. -->
    <Style x:Key="AppTextBoxStyle" TargetType="TextBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type TextBox}}">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource InteractiveEdgeBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    </Style>

    <!-- A password field's boundary matters more than most, not less: there is no text to infer the
         control's extent from, so the outline is the only thing saying where to type. -->
    <Style x:Key="AppPasswordBoxStyle" TargetType="PasswordBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type PasswordBox}}">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource InteractiveEdgeBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    </Style>

    <!-- IMPLICIT, deliberately, and the only implicit style in this file (F-049). The alerts block
         was added twenty lines below an identical ComboBox and picked up none of its theming,
         because nothing made it inherit. Keying this would preserve exactly that failure mode: the
         next ComboBox someone adds would again theme itself by hand or not at all. Buttons and text
         boxes stay keyed — they have more than one legitimate recipe, so an implicit default there
         would silently overrule deliberate choices. A ComboBox has one. -->
    <Style TargetType="ComboBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type ComboBox}}">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource NavyBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource InteractiveEdgeBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    </Style>

    <!-- ======================= SURFACES + HEADINGS (F-047, F-064) =======================

         WHAT THESE DO AND DO NOT FIX. They consolidate. Nine section cards in Preferences repeat
         the same four attributes by hand; Diagnostics builds its section heading in C# with a third
         spec no markup review would ever see. One style each, so the next change is one edit.

         They do NOT fix C5. A card is a fill and a radius, and under a theme that sets RowBg == Bg
         the grouping disappears entirely — no brush in this file can rescue that, because the
         problem is that grouping is carried by colour alone. The real fix is structural: a heading
         level above the card so the group reads from typography and rhythm rather than a fill. That
         is F-026, tagged [new-mechanism] in the register, and it is deliberately not smuggled in
         here. What this does is make F-026 a one-place edit instead of a nine-place one. -->

    <Style x:Key="CardBorderStyle" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource RowBgBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="8" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="14" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,10" />
    </Style>

    <!-- C8's stated convention, finally expressed once: 13px SemiBold on the primary text token.
         Diagnostics had it at 12px on CyanBrush — accent colour doing structural work, which is the
         same mistake as grouping-by-fill and fails for the same reader. The generous top margin is
         the point: with the accent gone, separation between groups has to come from rhythm, not
         hue. -->
    <Style x:Key="SectionHeadingStyle" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,18,0,6" />
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
